        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, drawn from current India job-board and salary-tracking sources at the time of
    writing. Some industry salary surveys report notably higher averages for experienced UX writers at large
    product companies. Verify current figures against live postings at your target companies before making a
    financial decision.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      UX writing pays meaningfully more than generalist content writing at the same experience level, once you
      clear the entry bar — a generalist content writer typically earns roughly Rs 2.5-4.5 LPA fresh out, well
      below UX writing's fresher range. But the trade-off is real: the employer pool is narrower, the entry bar
      is a genuine portfolio, and there are far fewer roles in absolute numbers than general content writing.
      This is a smaller, higher-paying lane, not a bigger one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="employers">Who hires UX writers in India</h2>

  <p>
    Knowing where these roles actually exist changes how you should target your applications.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {employerItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Both LinkedIn and Naukri list well over a thousand UX-writer-titled and closely related "content designer"
    or "user experience writer" openings in India at any given time, concentrated heavily at product-led
    companies rather than spread evenly across the whole job market. Treat that concentration as your targeting
    map, not a reason to apply broadly to every writing job you see.
  </p>

  <h2 id="portfolio">Building a portfolio with zero product experience</h2>

  <p>
    This is the actual bottleneck for most career-changers, and it is solvable without a job, an internship, or
    permission from anyone.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {portfolioItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Two to four strong, well-reasoned case studies covering different problem types beat ten shallow ones.
    Hiring managers are screening for how you think through a wording problem, not how many examples you can
    produce.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you spend a stretch of time on this</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol tells you whether this lane fits on paper. The 3 Gates make you test it in the
    real world before you invest a course fee or months of unpaid portfolio-building into it.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not enrol in a paid UX writing course or start job-hunting full time before passing all three gates.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>
          Rewrite one genuinely confusing screen from a real app you use, with your reasoning written out, laid
          out in a basic Figma file — not a polished portfolio piece yet, just a real first attempt.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
        <p>
          Explain your rewrite to someone in under two minutes: what was confusing, what you changed, and why
          it would reduce errors or hesitation for a real user.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>
          Show the case study to a working UX writer, content designer, or product designer and ask one direct
          question: "Would this get you an interview at your company?" Use their answer, not your own hope, to
          decide whether to keep building.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai-impact">How AI is changing this job</h2>

  <p>
    UX writing sits in an unusual position with AI compared to other writing careers. A generic AI tool can
    now draft a plausible button label, error message, or onboarding line in seconds, and many product teams
    already use AI to generate first-draft microcopy for review.
  </p>

  <p>
    What AI cannot reliably do is know which of several technically-correct wordings will actually reduce
    confusion or errors at that specific step, for that specific user base, inside that specific design
    system — that judgment comes from usability-test data, support-ticket patterns, and product context an AI
    tool does not have access to. The writers who stay valuable are the ones who own that testing and reasoning
    layer, not the ones who draft fastest.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      A practical way to future-proof this specific career: treat AI drafting tools as a first-draft generator
      you edit and validate, not a threat to route around. Learn to use AI to speed up the drafting of routine
      microcopy variants, then spend your saved time on the parts AI cannot do — user testing, cross-screen
      consistency, and defending decisions in a design critique.
    </p>
  </div>

